# Allowlist, not a denylist.
#
# Everything is excluded first and the few paths the image actually needs
# are added back. A denylist protects exactly the files someone thought of
# on the day they wrote it, and silently starts shipping whatever gets
# added to the repository root afterwards -- which, in a repository whose
# root already holds a .env, is the wrong default to pick.
*

# The five paths the runtime image cannot be built without, and the reason
# each one is here rather than mounted. Render has NO BIND MOUNTS: whatever
# the container needs at run time has to be inside it, so the three entries
# below `requirements.txt` are the ones docker-compose.yml used to supply
# from the host and no longer can.
#
#   app                the application package
#   requirements.txt   the pinned, hashed runtime lock the builder installs
#   migrations         the schema itself, applied at boot from an empty
#                      database -- the deployment target is a brand new Neon
#                      project, so 001 onwards has to be in the image
#   scripts            `python -m scripts.apply_migration`, which owns the
#                      ledger, the advisory lock and the per-file checksum.
#                      Nothing else may apply a migration, so nothing else
#                      would be safe to bake in instead
#   frontend           the sources the Node stage builds the bundle from, so
#                      that FastAPI can serve the SPA and the API on ONE
#                      origin. Two origins is what broke OAuth state before
#                      (see app/rest/oauth_origin.py) and is not repeated
!app
!requirements.txt
!migrations
!scripts
!frontend

# Re-excluded inside the allowed paths above: build artefacts are rebuilt
# by the interpreter and would otherwise bust the layer cache on every
# build and ship stale bytecode compiled by a different interpreter.
app/**/__pycache__
scripts/**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc

# Re-excluded inside `frontend`, and this list is `frontend/.dockerignore`
# restated. That file governs a build whose context is `frontend/`; this
# build's context is the repository root, so Docker never reads it and every
# exclusion it makes has to be made again here.
#
#   node_modules  installed by `npm ci` in the builder. A host copy would
#                 ship Windows-resolved esbuild and rollup binaries
#   dist          the previous build's output; copying a stale one in would
#                 let a successful-looking image serve last week's bundle
#   e2e           in no tsconfig, so `tsc -b && vite build` never reads it.
#                 Excluded for the layer cache: without this, editing a spec
#                 forces a full rebuild of a bundle that cannot have changed
frontend/node_modules
frontend/dist
frontend/e2e
frontend/playwright-report
frontend/test-results

# The paths that must never reach an image: local secrets, private keys, the
# developer virtualenv (Windows binaries, useless and large in a Linux
# image), version control history and test caches.
#
# `.env` and `.env.*` alone were enough while the allowlist was two files and
# `*` covered everything else. It is not enough now. Three of the five
# entries above are whole DIRECTORIES, so `*` no longer protects anything
# inside them -- a `.env` or a downloaded GitHub App `.pem` dropped into
# `scripts/` or `frontend/` would be copied into the image by a `COPY` that
# looks entirely innocent. The `**/` spellings are what close that, and
# tests/test_phase1a2_gates.py asserts every line in this block.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
.venv
.git
.pytest_cache
